  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Academy_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7f396f1f5ce534da68445c68f9fa4252, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 456122368
    m_Localized: CEO Academy
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705280
    m_Localized: Money in this game is simulated. This is not financial advice.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705281
    m_Localized: Keep playing to unlock this lesson.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705282
    m_Localized: Correct!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705283
    m_Localized: Not quite. Read the lesson again.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705284
    m_Localized: Quiz
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705285
    m_Localized: Revenue vs. profit
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705286
    m_Localized: 'Revenue is every dollar that comes in. Profit is what stays after you pay for everything it took to earn it: supplies, app fees, the bike, your phone, taxes. A courier can bring in $3,000 a month and keep far less once all of that is paid. Big companies live by the same math: in 2012 Amazon sold $61 billion of goods and still posted a small loss, because it poured almost everything back into growth. Rule of thumb: brag about revenue, plan with profit.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705287
    m_Localized: You earned $2,000 from gigs and spent $600 on costs. What is your profit before tax?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705288
    m_Localized: $2,000
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705289
    m_Localized: $1,400
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705290
    m_Localized: $2,600
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705291
    m_Localized: Which number pays your rent?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705292
    m_Localized: Profit
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705293
    m_Localized: Revenue
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705294
    m_Localized: The number of orders
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705295
    m_Localized: Revenue keeps growing, but the company is losing money. What is most likely true?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705296
    m_Localized: 'All is fine: revenue is all that matters'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705297
    m_Localized: It doesn't pay taxes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705298
    m_Localized: Its costs grow as fast as its sales, or faster
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705299
    m_Localized: Your reserve
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705300
    m_Localized: 'The first bills just hit. Rent, food and the subway fare come every month, whether you earned anything or not. A reserve is money you set aside only for the months when income doesn''t show up. Many advisors suggest building three to six months of expenses. Start smaller: even one month of bills is freedom, because it lets you say no to bad deals, like a loan shark''s. In this game the reserve sits apart from your cash, and you need one month of bills in it to finish chapter one.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705301
    m_Localized: Why keep the reserve apart from your spending money?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705302
    m_Localized: So it earns more interest
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705303
    m_Localized: So you don't spend it on everyday things
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705304
    m_Localized: Because the bank requires it
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705305
    m_Localized: Your bills are $1,700 a month. What is a solid first reserve goal?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705306
    m_Localized: $170
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705307
    m_Localized: $17,000 before you can start anything
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705308
    m_Localized: $1,700
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705309
    m_Localized: When is it right to spend the reserve?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705310
    m_Localized: When income drops and bills are due
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705311
    m_Localized: To catch a sale on a new phone
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705312
    m_Localized: Never, under any circumstances
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705313
    m_Localized: Reading a loss
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705314
    m_Localized: 'A loss means the month cost more than it earned. It is not a verdict, it is data. Open the P&L and ask three questions. Did sales drop, or did costs rise? Which cost grew fastest? Was it a one-time hit, like a repair, or will it repeat every month? Even giants post losses: Uber lost money for years before it reported its first full-year operating profit in 2023. The difference between a loss and a disaster is knowing where it came from.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705315
    m_Localized: Your month ended with a loss. What should you do first?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705316
    m_Localized: Find which costs grew faster than sales
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705317
    m_Localized: Quit the business
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705318
    m_Localized: Raise every price right away
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705319
    m_Localized: A $200 bike repair caused this month's loss. What kind of cost is it?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705320
    m_Localized: A recurring cost
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705321
    m_Localized: A one-time cost
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705322
    m_Localized: A tax
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705323
    m_Localized: Which statement is true?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705324
    m_Localized: Profitable companies never have losing months
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705325
    m_Localized: A loss means revenue was zero
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705326
    m_Localized: 'A loss is data: it shows what to fix'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705327
    m_Localized: Cash gap
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705328
    m_Localized: 'A cash gap happens when money has to go out before money comes in. Rent is due on the first, but a client pays in thirty days. On paper you are profitable; in your account you are negative. That is how profitable small businesses still go under: they run out of cash. Three tools close the gap: a reserve, getting paid sooner, and paying suppliers later on agreed terms. Expensive debt, like a loan shark''s, is the worst tool: it closes the gap today and opens a bigger one tomorrow.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705329
    m_Localized: You're profitable on paper, but your balance is negative. What is this called?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705330
    m_Localized: Bankruptcy
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705331
    m_Localized: A cash gap
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705332
    m_Localized: Inflation
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705333
    m_Localized: Which move closes a cash gap without new debt?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705334
    m_Localized: Asking customers to pay sooner
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705335
    m_Localized: A loan from Mr. Fins
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705336
    m_Localized: Buying more stock
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705337
    m_Localized: Why is a loan shark the worst way to close a gap?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705338
    m_Localized: It's slow
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705339
    m_Localized: He only lends small amounts
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705340
    m_Localized: The interest makes the next gap bigger
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705341
    m_Localized: Break-even point
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705342
    m_Localized: 'Break-even is the number of sales at which you stop losing money. The formula: fixed costs divided by what each sale leaves after its own cost. Say a food truck pays $3,000 a month for parking, insurance and its loan. A meal sells for $12 and costs $4 to make, so it leaves $8. $3,000 divided by $8 is 375 meals a month. Meal number 376 is the first that earns a profit. In Brooklyn you''ll have rent and staff, so know your break-even before you sign anything.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705343
    m_Localized: Fixed costs are $2,000 a month. Each sale leaves $10 after its own cost. What is the break-even?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705344
    m_Localized: 20 sales
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705345
    m_Localized: 200 sales
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705346
    m_Localized: 2,000 sales
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705347
    m_Localized: What happens after you pass break-even?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705348
    m_Localized: Each extra sale adds profit
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705349
    m_Localized: Costs disappear
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705350
    m_Localized: Taxes stop
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705351
    m_Localized: "Your supplier raises the cost of each item. Your break-even point\u2026"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705352
    m_Localized: goes down
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705353
    m_Localized: stays the same
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 468705354
    m_Localized: goes up
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748367134720
    m_Localized: Unit economics
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717632
    m_Localized: 'Before you buy the first cart, truck or van, look at a single sale. A coffee cart sells a cup for $4, and milk, beans and the cup cost $1.20. That leaves $2.80, a 70% gross margin. Fixed costs arrive every month whether anyone buys or not: $1,200 for the permit, parking and insurance needs 429 cups just to be covered. That is your break-even point. Unit economics asks three questions. What does one sale leave you? How many sales cover the month? What does a customer cost to win, and what do they bring over time? If one sale loses money, selling more only loses it faster. The Company screen shows these numbers for your business every month.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717633
    m_Localized: A cup sells for $4 and costs $1.20 to make. What is the gross margin?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717634
    m_Localized: 30%
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717635
    m_Localized: 70%
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717636
    m_Localized: $4
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717637
    m_Localized: Fixed costs are $1,200 a month and each sale leaves $2.80. How many sales do you need to break even?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717638
    m_Localized: 429
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717639
    m_Localized: 300
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717640
    m_Localized: 1,200
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717641
    m_Localized: Each sale loses 50 cents. What happens if you sell twice as much?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717642
    m_Localized: You break even sooner
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717643
    m_Localized: Profit doubles
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717644
    m_Localized: You lose money twice as fast
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717645
    m_Localized: Setting the price
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717646
    m_Localized: Price is the fastest lever you have, and the easiest to get wrong. Raise it and each sale leaves more, but some customers walk away. Cut it and more people buy, but each sale leaves less. A resale store sells jackets that cost it $15. At $40 it earns $25 a jacket; at $30 only $15, so it must sell about two-thirds more jackets just to earn the same. How strongly demand reacts to price is called elasticity. Coffee by a subway exit barely notices a 25-cent rise; a jacket next to a cheaper shop does. Change prices in small steps, watch a full month of sales, and remember that a discount is easy to start and hard to take back.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717647
    m_Localized: A jacket costs you $15. At $40 you earn $25 each, at $30 only $15. How many more jackets must you sell at $30 to earn the same?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717648
    m_Localized: About two-thirds more
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717649
    m_Localized: 10% more
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717650
    m_Localized: The same number
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717651
    m_Localized: What does elasticity measure?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717652
    m_Localized: How fast prices rise every year
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717653
    m_Localized: How much demand changes when the price changes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717654
    m_Localized: How stretchy your products are
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717655
    m_Localized: What is the safest way to test a new price?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717656
    m_Localized: Halve it for a week
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717657
    m_Localized: Copy the most expensive competitor
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717658
    m_Localized: Change it a little and watch a month of sales
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717659
    m_Localized: Profit means taxes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717660
    m_Localized: 'Your unit just covered its costs. Congratulations: now there is profit, and profit is taxed. An employee has tax taken out of every paycheck; an owner has to send it in. In the US, owners usually pay estimated taxes four times a year: by April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15. Self-employed people also owe self-employment tax, 15.3% for Social Security and Medicare on most of their profit, on top of income tax. Missed payments bring penalties and a painful bill in April. The habit that saves founders is simple: move a share of every month''s profit into a separate tax account and don''t touch it. The game sets aside a tax reserve for you each month; in real life nobody does it for you.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717661
    m_Localized: How often do US business owners usually pay estimated taxes?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717662
    m_Localized: Once, in April
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717663
    m_Localized: Four times a year
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717664
    m_Localized: Only when they sell the business
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717665
    m_Localized: What is the self-employment tax for Social Security and Medicare?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717666
    m_Localized: 15.3%
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717667
    m_Localized: 1.5%
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717668
    m_Localized: 50%
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717669
    m_Localized: Which habit keeps a tax bill from becoming a crisis?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717670
    m_Localized: Spend the profit now and pay later
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717671
    m_Localized: Ask customers to pay the tax
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717672
    m_Localized: Move part of each month's profit to a separate tax account
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717673
    m_Localized: Why an LLC
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717674
    m_Localized: 'Moving to Brooklyn means running a real business, and a real business needs a legal structure. As a sole proprietor, you and the business are the same person: if it owes money or gets sued, your own savings are on the line. A limited liability company, an LLC, puts a wall between the two. In New York, forming one costs $200 to file Articles of Organization with the Department of State. Then comes a New York quirk: within 120 days you must publish a notice in two newspapers chosen by the county clerk for six weeks in a row and file a $50 Certificate of Publication. In New York City the newspapers alone can cost over $1,000. Skip it, and the LLC loses its right to do business until you fix it.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717675
    m_Localized: What is the main point of an LLC?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717676
    m_Localized: Lower prices for customers
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717677
    m_Localized: It separates your personal money from the business's debts
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717678
    m_Localized: You never pay taxes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717679
    m_Localized: What does New York charge to file Articles of Organization for an LLC?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717680
    m_Localized: $200
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717681
    m_Localized: $20
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717682
    m_Localized: $2,000
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717683
    m_Localized: What happens if a New York LLC skips the publication requirement?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717684
    m_Localized: Nothing at all
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717685
    m_Localized: It becomes a corporation
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717686
    m_Localized: Its right to do business is suspended until it publishes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717687
    m_Localized: Your first hire
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717688
    m_Localized: 'Your first employee changes your job: now you lead, not only work. Hire for the gap that costs you the most, like the shifts you can''t cover or the calls you can''t take. In New York City the minimum wage in 2026 is $17 an hour, and hours over 40 a week are paid at time and a half. Before the first day you need an EIN, an I-9 form to check the right to work, tax forms, and workers'' compensation and disability insurance, and you report the new hire to New York State within 20 days. A good first hire pays for themselves by freeing your time for selling and planning; a wrong one costs months. Before you post the job, write down what success looks like after 30 days.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717689
    m_Localized: What is the minimum wage in New York City in 2026?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717690
    m_Localized: $7.25
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717691
    m_Localized: $17.00
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717692
    m_Localized: $25.50
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717693
    m_Localized: An employee works 45 hours one week at $18 an hour. How are the 5 extra hours paid?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717694
    m_Localized: At $27 an hour, time and a half
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717695
    m_Localized: At $18, like the rest
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717696
    m_Localized: They are not paid
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717697
    m_Localized: What should you write down before posting a job?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717698
    m_Localized: Your competitors' salaries
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717699
    m_Localized: A list of holidays
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717700
    m_Localized: What success looks like after 30 days
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717701
    m_Localized: The real cost of payroll
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717702
    m_Localized: 'An employee paid $3,000 a month costs you more than $3,000. On top of the salary, the employer pays 7.65% for Social Security and Medicare, plus federal and state unemployment insurance, workers'' compensation and disability insurance. Many small businesses budget 10 to 30% above wages for all of it. Payroll also keeps a schedule your customers don''t: salaries leave on a fixed day even when clients pay late. That is why payroll is the cost that most often turns a profitable month into an empty account. In the game, every hire adds the salary plus 10% overhead to your fixed costs. Before hiring, check that gross profit covers the full cost, not just the salary.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717703
    m_Localized: Besides the salary, what share does an employer pay for Social Security and Medicare?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 90748379717704
    m_Localized: 7.65%
    m_Metadata:
